=== SIGNATURE ===
ad_col_let(col_n_or_ad)

=== COMMENT ===
Column letters from a column number OR an address. A scalar integer 1..16384 becomes its column-letter string (1475 -> "BDS"); a valid numeric address (66000045) or A1 string address ("AA99") becomes its column's letters (-> "AS", "AA"); anything else is returned unchanged. Over an array, each cell is converted the same way.

=== CODE ===
=LAMBDA(col_n_or_ad,
  LET(
    col_to_let, LAMBDA(col, LET(a, ADDRESS(1, col, 4), LEFT(a, LEN(a) - 1))),
    addr_col, LAMBDA(one,
      LET(box, IFERROR(z_ad_box(one), 0),
        IF(COUNT(box) = 4,
          LET(top, SUM(INDEX(box, 1)), lft, SUM(INDEX(box, 2)),
              bot, SUM(INDEX(box, 3)), rgt, SUM(INDEX(box, 4)),
            IF(AND(top = bot, lft = rgt, top >= 1, lft >= 1), col_to_let(lft), one)),
          one))),
    to_let, LAMBDA(one,
      IF(ISNUMBER(one),
         IF(AND(one = INT(one), one >= 1, one <= 16384),
            col_to_let(one),
            addr_col(one)),
         addr_col(one))),
    IF(ROWS(col_n_or_ad) * COLUMNS(col_n_or_ad) > 1,
       MAP(col_n_or_ad, to_let),
       to_let(col_n_or_ad))
  )
)

=== DESCRIPTION ===
Column-letter string(s) from either a column number or an address. Companion to ad_row_n / ad_col_n, and the inverse of ad_col_n.

Behaviour (element-wise over an array):
  integer 1..16384          -> its column letters              (1 -> "A", 27 -> "AA", 1475 -> "BDS", 16384 -> "XFD")
  numeric address 1e6*r+c   -> its COLUMN's letters            (66000045 -> "AS", 1000001 -> "A")
  A1 string address         -> its COLUMN's letters            ("AA99" -> "AA", "$D$3" -> "D")
  anything else             -> returned unchanged              (0, 16385, 1.5, a range, plain text, a bare column letter)

Precedence: a whole number in 1..16384 is read as a column index first (that is the common case and what a column number looks like); only larger numbers are tried as numeric addresses, so 66000045 reads as row 66 / column 45, not as a column index. Addresses are parsed with the shared z_ad_box, and letters are built from ADDRESS(1, col, 4) with the trailing row digit dropped - no lookup table. A range is not a single address, so it passes through unchanged.

Arguments:
  col_n_or_ad - a column number, a single address (numeric or A1), or an array of such values.

Returns:
  A column-letter string, the array of per-element results, or the input unchanged where it is neither a 1..16384 integer nor a single address.

See also ad_col_n (address/letter -> column number), ad_row_n, rc_to_ad.
